<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- PART I: FOUNDATIONAL LAW CASES              -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #64748b;">
<span class="part-accent"></span>
	<div class="part-mark">
		<span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
		<span class="part-mark-label">Part I<span class="part-mark-label-sub">Foundational law cases</span></span>
	</div>
	<Section id="foundational-cases" eyebrow="Part I" title="Foundational Law Cases">
		<p class="para">These four cases form the bedrock of medical negligence law. They establish the objective standard of care, the temporal frame for judging practice, the heightened duty owed to the vulnerable, and the defence of genuine emergency. Every subsequent authority builds on, modifies, or qualifies their principles.</p>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Foundational" title="Paris v Stepney Borough Council [1951] — The Heightened Duty Principle">
		<p class="para">Mr Paris, an employee who had only one eye, was injured when a metal fragment struck his remaining eye, rendering him completely blind. His employer had provided goggles for other workers but not for him, arguing that the risk to any single worker was the same as for everyone else.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Paris v Stepney Borough Council [1951] AC 367, HL.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">The House of Lords held that an employer must take reasonable care in light of all circumstances, including the known vulnerability of a particular employee. The greater the potential harm, the greater the precautions required — even if the likelihood of the event occurring is no greater than for others.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">This principle is relevant to radiation protection and contrast administration. A pregnant radiographer who is known to be pregnant requires heightened dose protection. Similarly, if a patient has known renal impairment, the radiologist's duty to assess renal function before administering contrast iodine is heightened by the greater consequence of breach.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Foundational" title="Watt v Hertfordshire [1954] — The Emergency Defence">
		<p class="para">A fireman, Watt, was badly injured when he tried to lift a heavy jack to free a trapped colleague. His officer said "not to try it" but Watt persisted. The employer had only a small winch available and argued that the risks of sending for it outweighed the need for the heavier jack.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Watt v Hertfordshire [1954] 1 WLR 835, HL.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">The House of Lords held that in an emergency, the magnitude of the risk must be weighed against the urgency of the situation. Lord Radcliffe: "the size of a risk which must be guarded against so that a defendant does not breach his duty of care to another will vary with the circumstances of the case."</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">In trauma imaging, the radiologist must balance the urgency of identifying a life-threatening finding against the completeness of the examination. An emergency CT head for a head-injured patient who cannot be moved may not be negligent if the clinical question was solely intracranial. Where the clinical scenario is broad, omitting CT angiography for PE on a trauma patient may fail this balancing test.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Foundational" title="Bolam v Friern HMC [1957] — The Foundation">
		<p class="para">Mr Bolam was a psychiatric patient who received electroconvulsive therapy without muscle relaxants. He suffered fractures. The court held that where a doctor acts in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of medical opinion, he is not negligent even if other doctors would have acted differently.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee [1957] 1 WLR 582.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">This case established the single most important test in UK medical negligence law. It defined the standard of care as what a responsible body of medical practitioners would do. It protected medical professionals from the unreasonable expectations of lay judges and juries. The test stood unchallenged for nearly forty years.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Bolam remains the starting point for every radiology negligence claim. When a missed finding is alleged, the defence must show that a responsible body of radiologists would have made the same interpretation. The test protects genuine differences of opinion but does not protect errors that no reasonable radiologist would make.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Foundational" title="Nettleship v Weston [1971] — The Objective Standard">
		<p class="para">A case that, while not a radiology case, has shaped the entire landscape of clinical negligence law. Mrs Weston, a learner driver, lost control and injured her passenger, Nettleship. The Court of Appeal held that a learner driver must be judged against the standard of the competent experienced driver — ignorance of the skills required does not excuse failure.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Nettleship v Weston [1971] 2 QB 691, CA.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">This case was the first to establish that the standard of care in negligence is an <strong>objective</strong> one. A professional (or learner professional) cannot argue they were below average — they must be judged against the competent practitioner standard. This directly underpins Bolam.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">A newly qualified registrar interpreting their first CT brain is judged against the competent consultant standard. A trainee radiographer positioning a trauma series is judged against the competent level. The objective standard applies regardless of experience.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- PART II: NHS CLAIMS DATA                    -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #475569;">
<span class="part-accent"></span>
	<div class="part-mark">
		<span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
		<span class="part-mark-label">Part II<span class="part-mark-label-sub">NHS claims data</span></span>
	</div>
	<Section id="nhs-data" eyebrow="Part II" title="NHS Resolution — Claims Data">
		<p class="para">NHS Resolution publishes annual statistics on clinical negligence claims. The data shows where claims arise, which specialties are most frequently cited, and the evolving cost of litigation to the NHS. Radiology remains one of the most frequently involved specialties in clinical claims.</p>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Data" title="NHS Resolution — Annual Claims Statistics 2024">
		<p class="para">The 2024 report shows that radiology remains one of the most frequently cited specialties in claims, with 4.6% of all claims being radiological — matching general medicine as the second-largest specialty after obstetrics.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Source: NHS Resolution, Annual Report and Accounts 2023–24.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Key data</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Total NHS litigation cost: £3.6 billion (2018/19 data, referenced by Halligan et al.).</li>
					<li>Radiology claims have increased in volume and value over the past decade.</li>
					<li>Missed diagnosis is the most common allegation across all radiology claims.</li>
					<li>Abdominal radiology claims have a high rate of incidental pathology allegations (37%, per Halligan).</li>
					<li>Radiology is the second-largest specialty after obstetrics in claim volume.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Implications</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">The rising volume of radiology claims reflects both the increasing centrality of imaging in clinical decision-making and the growing sophistication of claimants' legal teams in identifying radiological errors. Expert radiological review is now a standard feature of clinical negligence claims involving any imaging modality.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

