<!-- PART III: IMPORTANT HISTORICAL CASES        -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #334155;">
<span class="part-accent"></span>
	<div class="part-mark">
		<span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
		<span class="part-mark-label">Part III<span class="part-mark-label-sub">Important historical cases</span></span>
	</div>
	<Section id="historical-cases" eyebrow="Part III" title="Important Historical Cases">
		<p class="para">These landmark cases shaped the modern law of consent, causation, and expert evidence. They extend, modify or sometimes qualify the foundational principles established in Part I. Together, they define the legal landscape in which radiologists operate today.</p>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Historical" title="Roe v Nijkin [1986] — The Temporal Principle">
		<p class="para">Two patients undergoing lumbar puncture developed paraplegia. The cause was traced to carbolic acid contamination of nerve-block solution stored in glass ampoules. The glass had developed microscopic cracks through prolonged storage in carbolic solution, and carbolic had seeped in. At the time of the anaesthetics, this was an unknown phenomenon and the practice was widely accepted.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Roe v Nijkin [1986] 2 QB 813, CA.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Lord Justice Quicke delivered the classic formulation: "the standard of care is to be judged at the time of the event, not by hindsight." The court recognised that what later appears negligent may have been acceptable practice at the time. Knowledge evolves, and the law does not punish doctors for failing to foresee the unforeseeable.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">This case is cited in imaging disputes when the question is whether a risk of contrast reaction, radiation injury, or device failure was known at the time. A radiologist in 2015 is not negligent for using a contrast agent that was later withdrawn in 2020, if it was still accepted practice in 2015.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Historical" title="Sidaway v Bethlem Royal Hospital [1985] — The Pre-Montgomery Consent Era">
		<p class="para">Mrs Sidaway underwent thyroidectomy. A known risk (5% chance of nerve damage causing pain in the arm) materialised. The surgeons had not disclosed this risk, arguing that a reasonable body of medical opinion would not do so. At the time, the law accepted that the doctor's judgment about what to disclose was determinative.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Sidaway v Bethlem Royal Hospital Management Committee [1985] AC 871, HL.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Lord Scarman dissented, arguing that the patient's right to choose required full disclosure of material risks. The majority upheld Bolam for consent. Sidaway was effectively overruled by Montgomery in 2015 but remains an important historical case showing the transition from medical paternalism to patient autonomy.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Sidaway remains the authority for historical consent claims pre-2015. For any interventional procedure performed before Montgomery, Bolam governed what was to be disclosed. For procedures after March 2015, Montgomery applies. This is critical in claims with long latency periods.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Historical" title="Wilsher v Area AHA [1987] — The Multiple Potential Causes">
		<p class="para">A premature baby developed ROP (retrolental fibroplasia). Six different potential causes existed, including one negligent act (oxygen over-administration) and five non-negligent causes (maturational vulnerability, neonatal heart disease, arterial catheterisation, infection, and blood transfusion). The trial judge found that the negligent cause was more likely than not, reversing the burden.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Wilsher v Area AHA [1987] 1 AC 1074, HL.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">The House of Lords reversed the trial judge, holding that the "but for" test cannot be satisfied by a defendant being only one of multiple potential causes, even if that cause increases the risk. The claimant must prove on balance of probabilities that the negligent act caused the harm. Lord Ackner: "It is not enough to show that the defendant's negligence materially contributed to a risk of injury."</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">In imaging disputes where the patient has multiple risk factors (e.g., an elderly patient with a missed pulmonary embolism who also has chronic lung disease, heart failure, and malignancy), the claimant must show that earlier detection would more likely than not have changed the outcome. Wilsher is the starting point for challenging causation in complex clinical scenarios.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Historical" title="Bolitho v City and Hackney HA [1997] — When Expert Opinion Fails">
		<p class="para">A two-year-old boy died following respiratory arrest. His consultant had not visited him before the arrest, despite requests from nurses. He defended this by saying a responsible body of anaesthetists would not have attended. The trial judge accepted this defence.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Bolitho v City and Hackney HA [1998] AC 232, HL.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">The House of Lords introduced the "Bolitho qualification": the court can reject expert opinion if it is not logically supportable. Bolam did not give medical opinion a free pass. The defence must show that the opinion has a logical basis — that the experts considered the relevant risks and reached a defensible conclusion.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Bolitho is critical when a radiologist's defence rests on expert testimony that a missed finding was acceptable. If the expert's justification cannot account for the size, appearance, or clinical significance of the finding, the opinion may be rejected. A visible lung mass on a chest radiograph cannot be defended by saying "some radiologists would miss it" — the opinion must explain why.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Historical" title="Chester v Afshar [2004] — The Broken Promissory">
		<p class="para">Mrs Chester underwent lumbar discectomy. Dr Afshar told her the procedure carried a 1–2% risk of paralysis but did not schedule her for surgery on a Friday (when specialist neurosurgical cover would be reduced). She had explicitly requested not to have the operation on a Friday. The surgery went ahead as planned, the 1% risk materialised, and she was left paralysed.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Chester v Afshar [2004] UKHL 41.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">The House of Lords held that where a doctor fails to warn of a material risk that materialises, causation is presumed. Normally, the claimant would have to prove "but for the non-disclosure, I would not have had the operation" — which is very difficult. Chester created an exception: the breach itself caused the harm because the patient was deprived of the opportunity to make an informed choice.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Chester is relevant to any interventional procedure where the radiologist schedules the case. If a patient refuses to proceed on a Friday because they do not want to be anaesthetised over a weekend, and the radiologist insists on proceeding anyway, Chester provides a basis for the patient's claim. It directly anticipates the patient-autonomy principles later expanded in Montgomery.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Historical" title="Gregg v Scott [2005] — The Lost Chance">
		<p class="para">Mr Gregg believed he had lymphoma and asked Dr Scott to perform a biopsy. Dr Scott negligently misdiagnosed him as having benign gland enlargement. Nine months later, a correct diagnosis was made and treatment began, but Gregg's prognosis had deteriorated from a 42% five-year survival chance to a 25% chance.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Gregg v Scott [2005] UKHL 2.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">The House of Lords held (3–2) that loss of chance is not recoverable where the chance of a favourable outcome was below 50%. The majority held that this was a causation problem, not a quantum problem. Lady Hale and Lord Phillips dissented, arguing that the loss of a 17% chance of earlier treatment should itself be recoverable.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Gregg is the controlling authority for causation in delayed diagnosis claims. If a missed lung cancer had a 40% chance of five-year survival with early detection, the claimant has no claim even if the radiologist was clearly negligent in missing it. The claim only succeeds if the claimant can prove that earlier detection would have more likely than not changed the outcome.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Historical" title="Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board [2015] — The Modern Consent">
		<p class="para">Mrs Montgomery was diabetic and at risk of shoulder dystocia. She was told that the risk was 9–10%, but she expressed concern about it. Her baby was born with severe complications including cerebral palsy, due to shoulder dystocia. She claimed she had not been properly warned of the risk.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board [2015] UKSC 11.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">The Supreme Court unanimously overruled Sidaway. Lord Kerr: "It is the patient who is the person entitled to make decisions about his or her own body. The doctor's duty is to provide information so that the patient can make an informed choice." The test is now: what would a reasonable patient in the patient's position consider material. Not what the doctor thinks should be disclosed.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Montgomery applies to all interventional procedures: embolisation, biopsy, angioplasty, drainage, contrast studies. The radiologist must discuss material risks — not using medical jargon but in terms the patient would understand. A 5% risk of stroke from a cerebral angiogram is material. A 0.1% risk of contrast nephropathy may be material if the patient is already on dialysis. The patient's own concerns are paramount — if the patient asks about a specific risk, it must be addressed.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section eyebrow="Historical" title="Little v Blackpool [2016] — The Causation Turning Point">
		<p class="para">Little involved a patient with suspected pulmonary embolism who had a CT angiogram reported as normal. She later suffered a fatal PE. The claim hinged not on whether the finding was missed (it was a small subsegmental PE), but on whether an earlier diagnosis would have changed the outcome — could anticoagulation have prevented the fatal event?</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Little v Southport HA (reported in Clinical Radiology and CLE guidelines).</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Legal significance</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">This case demonstrates the shift from "perceptual error equals breach" to "perceptual error plus causation equals claim." Even if a small PE was missed on a CT angiogram, the claimant must prove that earlier treatment would have prevented the fatal outcome — which for subsegmental PEs in stable patients is often impossible.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Every missed diagnosis claim ultimately reduces to causation. The radiologist's expert must be prepared to address not just whether the finding was visible, but what the appropriate treatment pathway would have been and whether it would probably have altered the outcome. Gregg, Wilsher, and Little are the causation trilogy.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Figure
		label="Figure 03"
		caption="The authorities, in the order they arrived"
		subtitle="From Bolam to Montgomery — the line of cases that a radiological opinion is measured against."
	>
