<!-- PART IV: APPLIED PRINCIPLES                  -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="part-bg" style="--accent: #0f172a;">
<span class="part-accent"></span>
	<div class="part-mark">
		<span class="part-mark-dot"></span>
		<span class="part-mark-label">Part IV<span class="part-mark-label-sub">Applied principles</span></span>
	</div>
	<Section id="standard-of-care" eyebrow="Part IV" title="Applied Principles: Standard of Care">
		<p class="para">The Bolam/Bolitho framework, refined by Nettleship's objective standard and Paris's heightened duty, defines how a radiologist's practice is assessed in litigation.</p>
	</Section>

	<Section id="standard-of-care-detail" index="01" eyebrow="Standard of care" title="Bolam Test: Bolam v Friern HMC [1957]">
		<p class="para">Test: practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of skilled practitioners is not negligent.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee [1957] 1 WLR 582.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Report judged against accepted practice at report date.</li>
					<li>Range of acceptable opinions permitted.</li>
					<li>Subtle findings below detection threshold protected.</li>
					<li>Demonstrable findings not protected.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Expert method</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Re-derive from source DICOM.</li>
					<li>Record protocol and image quality.</li>
					<li>Separate breach from causation.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section id="scrutiny" index="02" eyebrow="Scrutiny" title="Bolitho Qualification: Bolitho v City and Hackney HA [1997]">
		<p class="para">Test: expert opinion must have a logical basis. Illogical opinion can be rejected.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Bolitho v City and Hackney HA [1998] AC 232, HL.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Radiology application</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Where a visible lesion is missed, defence opinion requires logical justification. Assertion without analysis can be rejected.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Limit</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Genuine disagreement on subtle findings remains within Bolam. Bolitho applies to indefensible opinion only.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section id="consent" index="03" eyebrow="Consent" title="Montgomery v Lanarkshire [2015]">
		<p class="para">Test: disclose risks a reasonable patient in this position would consider material.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authority: Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board [2015] UKSC 11.</p>
		<div class="mt-6 divider pt-5">
			<h3 class="entry-title">Imaging procedures</h3>
			<ul class="list">
				<li>Applies to embolisation, biopsy, angioplasty, drainage, contrast studies with material risk.</li>
				<li>Requires discussion of indication, alternatives, procedural risks, dose and sedation.</li>
				<li>Requires documented discussion. Signature alone is insufficient.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section id="causation" index="04" eyebrow="Causation" title="Chester v Afshar [2004] and Gregg v Scott [2005]">
		<p class="para">Standard: but for breach, would outcome have differed on balance of probabilities.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Authorities: Chester v Afshar [2004] UKHL 41. Gregg v Scott [2005] UKHL 2.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Chester</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Non-disclosure of a material risk that materialised gave liability. Relevant to interventional consent.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Gregg</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Loss of chance below 50 percent is not recoverable. Delayed diagnosis requires proof that earlier action would probably have changed outcome.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section id="interpretation" index="05" eyebrow="Breach" title="Breach of Duty in Image Interpretation">
		<p class="para">Breach turns on demonstrability on the acquired study under standard viewing conditions.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Error types</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Perceptual: visible finding not detected.</li>
					<li>Cognitive: finding detected, incorrectly characterised.</li>
					<li>Published miss rates do not define breach.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Double reading</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Required in defined screening programmes only. Not a general diagnostic standard.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Consensus</h3>
				<p class="para-sm">Assessed on protocol, quality, clinical question and contemporaneous context. Retrospective certainty is not the test.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section id="reporting-standards" index="06" eyebrow="Regulatory framework" title="RCR Standards for Interpretation and Reporting (3rd Edition, October 2025)">
		<p class="para">The Royal College of Radiologists publishes the definitive standards governing how imaging investigations must be interpreted and reported across the NHS and private sector. The 3rd edition (October 2025) supersedes the 2018 edition and carries particular medicolegal weight because claimants routinely cite the standards to establish the expected standard of reporting practice.</p>
		<p class="para-sm">Reference: Royal College of Radiologists. Standards for interpretation and reporting of imaging investigations. 3rd ed. London: RCR, October 2025. <a href="https://www.rcr.ac.uk/our-services/all-our-publications/clinical-radiology-publications" class="link" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rcr.ac.uk</a></p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Key requirements</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Clinical details must form part of the report. Interpretation may be incorrect if referral details are deficient, absent or misleading — inclusion of the referrer's text is recommended.</li>
					<li>All images acquired, including planning images and non-target areas, must be reviewed to ensure patient safety. Responsibility lies with both reporter and employer.</li>
					<li>Incidental findings should be mentioned and explained, but irrelevant incidental findings that detract from the report's impact should be avoided.</li>
					<li>The report conclusion must directly answer the clinical question, be tailored to the named referrer, and avoid blanket phrases such as "clinical correlation advised".</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Medicolegal significance</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Standards are cited by claimants as evidence of the expected standard of care, even when the alleged pathology is incidental to the clinical question.</li>
					<li>The 2025 edition explicitly recognises that interpretation may be incorrect if referral details are deficient — a potential protection when clinical context is absent.</li>
					<li>The 2025 edition assigns responsibility for reviewing non-target areas to both reporter and employer, providing a shared-accountability defence.</li>
					<li>AI-assisted content must be clearly identified and verified by a named human author, who is responsible for the final report.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section id="candour" index="07" eyebrow="Communication" title="Duty of Candour and Escalation Protocols">
		<p class="para">The General Medical Council's guidance on the duty of candour (updated 2023) applies equally to all doctors, including radiologists. When something that has happened in connection with the care provided is or may have caused patient harm, the doctor must personally and officially report it. This extends to imaging errors — a missed malignancy, an incorrect laterality annotation, or a contrast reaction identified after report release.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Practical obligations</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Timely acknowledgment of the error to the patient or referrer.</li>
					<li>Apology — an apology is not an admission of liability but is a professional obligation.</li>
					<li>Written record of the apology and the circumstances.</li>
					<li>Review and learning — the organisation must investigate and share lessons.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Addenda and corrections</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Controlled delay (up to 2 minutes) before report accessibility, per RCR guidance, allows typographical corrections.</li>
					<li>Once accessed by another party, changes require formal version control with clear documentation of the amendment and notification to all relevant users.</li>
					<li>Addenda should supplement — not replace — the original report.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section id="ai-reporting" index="08" eyebrow="Technology" title="AI-Assisted Reporting: Liability and Medicolegal Implications">
		<p class="para">Artificial intelligence tools are now being deployed across UK radiology services — from triage algorithms prioritising critical findings to AI-generated structured report templates. The RCR's October 2025 standards set clear boundaries: validated AI tools must be integrated appropriately, AI-generated content must be clearly identified, and a named human author is responsible for the final report. All AI-derived contributions must be reviewed and approved before release.</p>
		<div class="entry-list">
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Medicolegal risks</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>AI-generated report formats must not imply a degree of scrutiny that has not taken place — this is a specific warning in the 2025 RCR Standards.</li>
					<li>Template-driven AI output may create an "expected line of sight" bias, increasing the risk of cognitive errors for findings outside the template structure.</li>
					<li>The named human author bears full responsibility — delegation of diagnostic judgment to an unvalidated or drift-affected algorithm may constitute breach.</li>
					<li>AI hallucination (fabricated findings) is a novel error type with no established precedent but would be assessed under standard Bolam/Bolitho principles.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="entry">
				<h3 class="entry-title">Defensible practice</h3>
				<ul class="list">
					<li>Document that an AI tool was used and which sections it generated.</li>
					<li>Record the validation status of the local AI deployment (the employer's duty to provide adequate training, per RCR 2025).</li>
					<li>Ensure the AI does not replace clinical context integration — the report must still answer the specific clinical question.</li>
					<li>Maintain the same quality assurance protocols (REALMs, discrepancy audits) for AI-assisted reporting as for conventional reporting.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</Section>

